# How Attribution Reportedly Developed

Investigators reportedly did not identify Farace through a single wallet; they identified him through control.

The blockchain reportedly showed where the money moved.

Exchange records reportedly helped identify who controlled the accounts receiving those funds.

Viewed individually, each source provided only part of the picture.

Viewed collectively, they reportedly helped investigators connect cryptocurrency proceeds to a specific individual.

---

# Why This Case Matters

Many discussions surrounding cryptocurrency focus on whether transactions can be traced.

This case highlights a different question:

**Can investigators determine who controls the funds?**

Blockchain analysis can reveal transaction flows.

Exchange records can reveal account ownership.

When combined, those sources can provide powerful attribution opportunities.

---

# Investigative Takeaways

Several observations stand out:

- A wallet address is not an identity.
- Blockchain activity often serves as an investigative lead.
- Exchange exposure remains one of the strongest attribution opportunities in cryptocurrency investigations.
- KYC records can provide the bridge between blockchain activity and real-world identity.
- Complex investigations are often solved through evidence correlation rather than a single breakthrough.

Many blockchain investigations seem to begin the same way:

Not with a suspect or a confession, but with financial relationships that keep pointing back to the same person.

The same lesson appears again and again across financial crime investigations:

Money can move through thousands of wallets, but ownership is much harder to hide.

